  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 310 PM Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...  A transition to cooler, wetter weather is expected \nthis weekend as a cold front moves through, with periods of rain \ncontinuing through Sunday.\n\nSaturday morning, a cold front will approach the region and slowly \nmove through during the day before stalling to our south. This will \nlikely allow for a large range in maximum temperatures for tomorrow, \nwith the forecast ranging from the low 70s in the north to the mid \n80s in the south. During the afternoon, showers with a few embedded \nthunderstorms look to develop mostly in the southern half of the \nregion ahead of and along the front. Another area of rain looks to \ndevelop behind the front to our north. This rain however looks like \nit should remain to our north according to latest CAM guidance. \n\nAs the front stalls to our south Saturday night, cool high pressure \nshould build in. This may result in a CAD wedge to set up over the \nregion. During the day on Sunday, the front looks like it could \nretrograde north, back into central NC. These two things should \ncombine to allow for a more widespread and steadier rain to develop \nSaturday night and through the day on Sunday. Most model and \nensemble guidance is suggesting between 1-2.5 inches of rain between \nSaturday morning and Monday morning, with the highest amounts in the \nsoutheast. Some models are also showing signals of a few locations \nwith higher rainfall totals where heavier rain could develop, mostly \nin the south near the frontal boundary.\n\nA reinforcing cold front will move through the region on Monday. \nThis should eliminate any rain chances for the rest of the week as \ncool and dry high pressure builds back in. High temperatures to \nstart the work week should generally be in the mid/upper 60s to mid \n70s, with Tuesday looking to be the coolest day. Lows look to drop \ninto the 40s Tuesday night as well.",
